      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><code>topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita</code> In the <strong>Explorer</strong>, right-click the <code>topics</code> folder, choose <strong>New File</strong>, and enter <code>what-is-digital-audio.dita</code>. Choose the <strong>Concept</strong> template and click <strong>OK</strong>. Replace the title placeholder, type the short description, and start the body in the empty <code>&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</code>. If you use another editor, create the file and type the finished listing.
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE concept PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Concept//EN\" \"concept.dtd\">\n\n<concept id=\"what-is-digital-audio\">\n  <title>What is digital audio?</title>\n  <shortdesc>Digital audio is sound stored as numbers: samples taken thousands of times a second at a chosen precision.</shortdesc>\n  <conbody>\n    <p>Digital audio is sound that has been converted into a numerical representation.\n    A few key concepts help you make better recordings and edits.</p>\n    <section>\n      <title>Waveforms</title>\n      <p>Sound travels through air as a continuous <term>waveform</term> of pressure changes.\n      A microphone converts these pressure changes into an electrical signal.\n      To store the signal digitally, the computer takes thousands of measurements, called <term>samples</term>, of the signal's amplitude every second.</p>\n    </section>\n    <section>\n      <title>Sample rate</title>\n      <p>The <term>sample rate</term> is the number of samples captured per second, measured in hertz (Hz).\n      Common sample rates are:</p>\n      <sl>\n        <sli>44,100 Hz: CD quality, enough for most audio work</sli>\n        <sli>48,000 Hz: the standard for video production</sli>\n        <sli>96,000 Hz: used in professional music production</sli>\n      </sl>\n      <p>A higher sample rate captures more detail but produces larger files.<fn>File size grows in proportion to the sample rate: a 96 kHz file is more than twice the size of the same recording at 44.1 kHz.</fn></p>\n    </section>\n    <section>\n      <title>Bit depth</title>\n      <p><term>Bit depth</term> determines how precisely each sample's amplitude is recorded.</p>\n      <simpletable>\n        <sthead>\n          <stentry>Bit depth</stentry>\n          <stentry>Amplitude values</stentry>\n          <stentry>Typical use</stentry>\n        </sthead>\n        <strow>\n          <stentry>16-bit</stentry>\n          <stentry>65,536</stentry>\n          <stentry>CD audio</stentry>\n        </strow>\n        <strow>\n          <stentry>24-bit</stentry>\n          <stentry>over 16 million</stentry>\n          <stentry>professional recording</stentry>\n        </strow>\n        <strow>\n          <stentry>32-bit float</stentry>\n          <stentry>effectively unlimited</stentry>\n          <stentry>editing inside Audacity</stentry>\n        </strow>\n      </simpletable>\n    </section>\n    <section>\n      <title>Key audio terms</title>\n      <dl>\n        <dlentry>\n          <dt>Amplitude</dt>\n          <dd>The height of a sound wave, corresponding to how loud the sound is.\n          Measured in decibels (dB).</dd>\n        </dlentry>\n        <dlentry>\n          <dt>Clipping</dt>\n          <dd>Distortion that occurs when the signal exceeds the maximum amplitude the system can represent.\n          It appears as flat-topped waveforms.</dd>\n        </dlentry>\n        <dlentry>\n          <dt>Mono and stereo</dt>\n          <dd>Mono audio uses a single channel.\n          Stereo uses two channels, left and right, to create a sense of width.</dd>\n        </dlentry>\n      </dl>\n      <lq reftitle=\"The Audacity Manual\">Audio is measured in decibels because the ear hears ratios, not differences.</lq>\n    </section>\n  </conbody>\n</concept>"} lang="xml" title="topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the inline elements</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;term&gt;</code> marks a word at the point where it is being defined or used as a defined term. Stage 12 links terms to glossary entries; marking them now is what makes that possible.</li>
      <li>Choose markup that describes the text. For example, use <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code> for a control label and leave a measurement such as "44,100 Hz" as text.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the block elements</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;sl&gt;</code> with <code>&lt;sli&gt;</code> is a simple list: short items, one line each, no bullets in most outputs. Use <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> when items are sentences.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;fn&gt;</code> is a footnote. It sits inline where the reference goes, and output moves the text to the foot of the page and leaves a marker.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;simpletable&gt;</code> uses <code>&lt;sthead&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;strow&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;stentry&gt;</code> for a regular grid. It supports column headers, <code>@keycol</code> for row headers, and <code>@relcolwidth</code> for relative widths, such as <code>relcolwidth="1* 2*"</code>. Use CALS <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> when you need spanning or a table title. See the <a href="https://docs.oasis-open.org/dita/dita/v1.3/os/part2-tech-content/langRef/attributes/simpletableAttributes.html">simple-table attributes</a>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;dl&gt;</code> is a definition list: each <code>&lt;dlentry&gt;</code> pairs a <code>&lt;dt&gt;</code> (the term) with a <code>&lt;dd&gt;</code> (its definition). It is the element for a list of named things with descriptions, which a <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> of "Name: description" items would leave unmarked.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;lq&gt;</code> is a long quotation, set off as a block. <code>@reftitle</code> names the source.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-2-write-the-task" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 2: Write the task<a href="#step-2-write-the-task" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><code>topics/trimming-audio.dita</code> Create <code>trimming-audio.dita</code> in the <code>topics</code> folder in the same way, from the <strong>Task</strong> template. Replace the title placeholder, type the short description, and fill in the steps starting from the empty <code>&lt;cmd&gt;&lt;/cmd&gt;</code>. If you use another editor, create the file and type the finished listing.
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE task PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Task//EN\" \"task.dtd\">\n\n<task id=\"trimming-audio\">\n  <title>Trimming audio</title>\n  <shortdesc>Remove silence, false starts and mistakes by selecting a region of the waveform and deleting it.</shortdesc>\n  <taskbody>\n    <context>\n      <p>Trimming removes unwanted audio from the start, end or middle of a track.\n      When you delete a region, the audio on either side closes the gap.</p>\n      <note type=\"tip\">Zoom in with <uicontrol>Ctrl+1</uicontrol> to make precise selections; zoom back out with <uicontrol>Ctrl+3</uicontrol>.</note>\n    </context>\n    <steps>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Click and drag in the waveform to select the region you want to remove.</cmd>\n        <info>\n          <p>The selected region is highlighted.\n          To select the whole track, press <uicontrol>Ctrl+A</uicontrol> (Windows and Linux) or <uicontrol>Cmd+A</uicontrol> (macOS).</p>\n        </info>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Press <uicontrol>Delete</uicontrol>, or choose <menucascade><uicontrol>Edit</uicontrol><uicontrol><shortcut>D</shortcut>elete</uicontrol></menucascade>.</cmd>\n        <info>\n          <note type=\"important\">Deleting changes the audio in the project.\n          Undo with <uicontrol>Ctrl+Z</uicontrol> if you removed the wrong region.</note>\n        </info>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>To keep only the selection and discard everything else, choose <menucascade><uicontrol>Edit</uicontrol><uicontrol>Remove Special</uicontrol><uicontrol>Trim Audio</uicontrol></menucascade> instead.</cmd>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Play the track from just before the edit to check the join sounds natural.</cmd>\n        <info>\n          <p>If you hear a click at the join, select a few milliseconds around it and apply <menucascade><uicontrol>Effect</uicontrol><uicontrol>Fade In</uicontrol></menucascade> or <uicontrol>Fade Out</uicontrol>.</p>\n        </info>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Save the project with <menucascade><uicontrol>File</uicontrol><uicontrol>Save Project</uicontrol><uicontrol>Save Project</uicontrol></menucascade>.</cmd>\n        <info>\n          <p>Audacity saves the project as a single <filepath>.aup3</filepath> file in the folder you choose; the <wintitle>Save Project</wintitle> dialog suggests your Documents folder.</p>\n        </info>\n      </step>\n    </steps>\n    <result>\n      <p>The unwanted audio is gone and the track plays through without a gap.</p>\n    </result>\n  </taskbody>\n</task>"} lang="xml" title="topics/trimming-audio.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the user-interface elements</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code> is the name of a button, menu item, field or key as it appears in the interface: <code>Delete</code>, <code>Ctrl+A</code>, <code>Edit</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;menucascade&gt;</code> is a path through menus. Its children are <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code> elements in order, and output joins them with an arrow: <strong>Edit &gt; Remove Special &gt; Trim Audio</strong>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;shortcut&gt;</code> marks the accelerator letter inside a control name, the underlined letter in a menu: <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;&lt;shortcut&gt;D&lt;/shortcut&gt;elete&lt;/uicontrol&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;wintitle&gt;</code> is the title of a window or dialog: the <em>Save Project</em> dialog.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;filepath&gt;</code> is a file name, path or extension: <code>.aup3</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the notes</strong>
      <ul><li><code>&lt;note&gt;</code> is a block that stands out from the text. <code>@type</code> says why: <code>tip</code> for a shortcut or a better way, <code>important</code> for something the reader must not skip, <code>warning</code> when something can go wrong, <code>caution</code> for a milder version, <code>note</code> (the default) for anything else. Stage 20 adds <code>trouble</code>. A note may sit in <code>&lt;context&gt;</code>. Inside a step, a note is allowed before the <code>&lt;cmd&gt;</code>. After the command, put the note inside <code>&lt;info&gt;</code>.</li></ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p><code>&lt;shortcut&gt;</code> is only valid inside <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code> in DITA 1.3. It marks the accelerator letter of a control, not a key combination. Key combinations such as <code>Ctrl+A</code> are plain <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code> text. If you write <code>&lt;cmd&gt;Press &lt;shortcut&gt;Delete&lt;/shortcut&gt;&lt;/cmd&gt;</code>, the check names the file and stops at the health stage. The <strong>Project Validation</strong> panel, or <code>dogsbay-xml project-health .</code>, gives the line, column, and message. The following output is an example:</p>
          <CodeBlock code={"health   NOT CLEAN\n  invalid: /home/you/audacity-guide/topics/trimming-audio.dita\n  (run project-health for the full report)\nNot ready: stopped at health — the project itself has faults, so nothing was built and no output was read."} lang="plaintext" />
          <CodeBlock code={"Root map: audacity-guide.ditamap (project config); house rules: none (none configured)\nInvalid files (1 of 6):\n  /home/you/audacity-guide/topics/trimming-audio.dita:\n    22:170  error: The content of element type \"cmd\" does not match its content model.\n\nSummary\n  Invalid files                 1  of 6"} lang="plaintext" />
          <p>If you try this, undo the change and check again.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <h2 id="step-3-mark-up-the-stage-04-topics" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 3: Mark up the stage 04 topics<a href="#step-3-mark-up-the-stage-04-topics" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><code>topics/recording-your-first-track.dita</code> The control names become <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code>, a tip goes into the context, and the sentence about the flat line becomes a <code>&lt;note type="warning"&gt;</code> inside the step's <code>&lt;info&gt;</code>.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\n+++ b/topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\n@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@\n     <context>\n       <p>This task walks you through making your first audio recording.\n       You need a microphone connected to your computer.</p>\n+      <note type=\"tip\">For best results, record in a quiet room and position the microphone 15 to 30 cm from your mouth.</note>\n     </context>\n     <steps>\n       <step>\n@@ -17,27 +18,28 @@\n         </info>\n       </step>\n       <step>\n-        <cmd>Select your microphone from the recording device list in the Device Toolbar.</cmd>\n+        <cmd>Select your microphone from the recording device list in the <uicontrol>Device Toolbar</uicontrol>.</cmd>\n         <info>\n-          <p>The Device Toolbar is near the top of the window.\n+          <p>The <uicontrol>Device Toolbar</uicontrol> is near the top of the window.\n           The recording device list has a microphone icon next to it.</p>\n         </info>\n       </step>\n       <step>\n-        <cmd>Click Record to start recording.</cmd>\n+        <cmd>Click <uicontrol>Record</uicontrol>, or press <uicontrol>R</uicontrol>, to start recording.</cmd>\n         <info>\n-          <p>A blue waveform appears as Audacity captures audio from your microphone.\n-          A flat line means the microphone is not selected correctly: click Stop and check the recording device.</p>\n+          <p>A blue waveform appears as Audacity captures audio from your microphone.</p>\n+          <note type=\"warning\">A flat line instead of a waveform means the microphone is not selected correctly.\n+          Click <uicontrol>Stop</uicontrol> and check the recording device.</note>\n         </info>\n       </step>\n       <step>\n         <cmd>Speak into your microphone, or play the audio you want to capture.</cmd>\n       </step>\n       <step>\n-        <cmd>Click Stop when you are finished.</cmd>\n+        <cmd>Click <uicontrol>Stop</uicontrol> when you are finished.</cmd>\n       </step>\n       <step>\n-        <cmd>Click Play to listen to your recording.</cmd>\n+        <cmd>Click <uicontrol>Play</uicontrol> to listen to your recording.</cmd>\n       </step>\n     </steps>\n     <result>"} lang="diff" title="topics/recording-your-first-track.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><code>topics/supported-audio-formats.dita</code> The reference gains a second section with an ordered list, a command name and a code block, and the terms and file names are marked.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\n+++ b/topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\n@@ -7,7 +7,19 @@\n   <refbody>\n     <section>\n       <p>Audacity imports and exports audio in several formats.\n-      Lossless formats keep every sample of the source; lossy formats discard detail to make smaller files.</p>\n+      <term>Lossless</term> formats keep every sample of the source; <term>lossy</term> formats discard detail to make smaller files.\n+      Exported files are named after the project, so <filepath>podcast-episode-1.aup3</filepath> exports as <filepath>podcast-episode-1.mp3</filepath>.</p>\n+    </section>\n+    <section>\n+      <title>Choosing a format</title>\n+      <p>Keep a lossless master and export a lossy copy for distribution:</p>\n+      <ol>\n+        <li>Save the project (<filepath>.aup3</filepath>) while you are still editing.</li>\n+        <li>Export a WAV or FLAC file as the archive copy.</li>\n+        <li>Export an MP3 or OGG file to share.</li>\n+      </ol>\n+      <p>On Linux you can convert between formats afterwards with <cmdname>ffmpeg</cmdname>:</p>\n+      <codeblock>ffmpeg -i episode.wav -b:a 128k episode.mp3</codeblock>\n     </section>\n     <table>\n       <title>Common audio formats</title>\n@@ -37,6 +49,7 @@\n             <entry>Compressed</entry>\n             <entry>Lossy</entry>\n             <entry>Widely supported, small files.\n+            Needs the <i>LAME</i> encoder, bundled since version 2.3.2.\n             Good for sharing and the web.</entry>\n           </row>\n           <row>"} lang="diff" title="topics/supported-audio-formats.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the new elements</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> with <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> is an ordered list. In a concept or reference it is for a sequence that is not a procedure; a procedure belongs in a task's <code>&lt;steps&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;cmdname&gt;</code> is the name of a command-line program. <code>&lt;codeph&gt;</code> (in the next stage) is a fragment of code inline; <code>&lt;codeblock&gt;</code> is a block of code, lines and spaces preserved. <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> is the same for preformatted text that is not code.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;i&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;b&gt;</code> exist, and <em>LAME</em> uses one because the encoder's name is conventionally set in italics. That is the case for them: typographic convention with no semantic element. When there is a semantic element, <code>&lt;term&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;cmdname&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;filepath&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;ph&gt;</code>, use it instead; <code>&lt;b&gt;</code> says nothing a stylesheet or a translator can use.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;ph&gt;</code> is a phrase with no more specific meaning. It carries attributes (conditions in stage 14, ids for reuse in stage 11) when you need a handle on a run of text and none of the other inline elements fits.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-4-update-the-map-and-the-readme-and-check-your-work" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 4: Update the map and the README, and check your work<a href="#step-4-update-the-map-and-the-readme-and-check-your-work" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Change the "You are on" line</strong>
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/README.md\n+++ b/README.md\n@@ -2,7 +2,7 @@\n \n Build a guide through 27 stages. Maps begin at stage 02; HTML publication begins at stage 03.\n \n-You are on stage 04: task and reference.\n+You are on stage 05: inline and block.\n \n ## Stages\n "} lang="diff" title="README.md" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Add the topics to the map</strong> Add a <code>&lt;topicref&gt;</code> for each new topic to <code>audacity-guide.ditamap</code>. Do this before you check your work: the check reports a topic that no map refers to as an orphan topic. The complete map at this checkpoint is:
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE map PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Map//EN\" \"map.dtd\">\n\n<map>\n  <title>Audio user guide</title>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\"/>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\"/>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/trimming-audio.dita\"/>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/what-is-audacity.dita\"/>\n  <topicref href=\"topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\"/>\n</map>"} lang="xml" title="audacity-guide.ditamap" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Format and check your work</strong> Format the topics:
      <CodeBlock code={"dogsbay-xml format -i topics/*.dita"} lang="bash" />
      <p>Then choose <strong>Project</strong> &gt; <strong>Check Project</strong> in the editor, or run <code>dogsbay-xml check .</code> from the project root. The check rebuilds the guide in <code>out/full/</code>. The output looks like this example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"health   clean\nbuild    full                 ok  /home/you/audacity-guide/out/full\noutput   clean (full)\nReady: the project is healthy, every deliverable built, and the output of full holds together."} lang="plaintext" />
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="what-you-learned" class="group scroll-mt-20">What you learned<a href="#what-you-learned" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <ul>
      <li>Inline elements name what text is: <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;menucascade&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;shortcut&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;wintitle&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;filepath&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;term&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;cmdname&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;b&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;i&gt;</code> are for typographic convention only; prefer a semantic element.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;note&gt;</code> has types. In a step, a note goes before the <code>&lt;cmd&gt;</code> or inside <code>&lt;info&gt;</code> after it.</li>
      <li>Lists: <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;sl&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;dl&gt;</code>; each is a different kind of list.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;simpletable&gt;</code> for short values, CALS <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> for everything else.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;fn&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;lq&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;codeblock&gt;</code> for footnotes, quotations and code.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;shortcut&gt;</code> only inside <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code>.</li>
      </ul>
